#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167722} :activity/id #uuid "775b7c3e-3e96-5ea8-a95f-883523a63b42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423 :topic/id #uuid "f3e0d484-510c-5c15-beb2-bef21881c55b" :topic/math-academy-id 3235} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168109 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis with acceleration $a(t),$ velocity $v(t)$ and displacement $x(t),$ where $t$ is the time. If we want to calculate the acceleration of $P,$ we use the relationship \n\n$$\na(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we know the acceleration and we want to find the velocity, we use the relationship\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\int a(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nIn both of these relationships, we require $a$ and $v$ to be expressed as functions of $t.$ \n\nNow suppose that we're given the acceleration as a function of the displacement $x.$ How do we calculate the velocity in this case?\n\nFor example, suppose that the acceleration of a particle is given by $a(x) = 4x\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$ A sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/01/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ equals $4x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite the left-hand side of this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ by definition, we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nAlso, by the chain rule for differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = 4x.\n$$\n\nBy solving this differential equation, we can find an expression for the velocity $v$ in terms of displacement $x.$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "291c6fee-a1ab-554d-b0b8-5508bd23eb2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5369 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "af56a6fb-d731-5179-b8b7-de268aeb13f2" :step/math-academy-id 33520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167727}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166853 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166705 :question/id #uuid "e8353dba-48d7-5f4f-8ba5-97e4e22b6e44" :question/math-academy-id "e-9050"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378470}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "ea23ed7c-763e-54fa-af53-bdcd85190355" :step/math-academy-id 33782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166706 :question/id #uuid "49e02115-6211-564b-8221-d5835a98a064" :question/math-academy-id "e-9051"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}